{"subjectId":"sql","id":"triggers","title":"Triggers","group":"Views, Indexes & Programmability","order":44,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A trigger is code the database runs <strong>automatically</strong> before or after an <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code> on a table — for audit logs, derived columns and integrity rules.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Options</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Timing</td><td><code>BEFORE</code>, <code>AFTER</code>, <code>INSTEAD OF</code> (views)</td></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td><code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE [OF col]</code>, <code>DELETE</code></td></tr><tr><td>Granularity</td><td><code>FOR EACH ROW</code> (row-level) or per statement (PostgreSQL, SQL Server)</td></tr><tr><td>Row values</td><td><code>NEW.col</code> (insert/update), <code>OLD.col</code> (update/delete); SQL Server uses <code>inserted</code>/<code>deleted</code> tables</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-history\"></i>Audit log with AFTER UPDATE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE salary_audit (\n    emp_id     INTEGER,\n    old_salary INTEGER,\n    new_salary INTEGER,\n    changed_at TEXT DEFAULT '2024-06-01'\n);\n\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_salary_audit\nAFTER UPDATE OF salary ON employees\nFOR EACH ROW\nWHEN NEW.salary &lt;&gt; OLD.salary\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO salary_audit (emp_id, old_salary, new_salary)\n    VALUES (OLD.emp_id, OLD.salary, NEW.salary);\nEND;\n\nUPDATE employees SET salary = salary + 5000 WHERE dept_id = 3;\nUPDATE employees SET name = 'Sara K' WHERE emp_id = 9;       -- salary not touched: no audit row\n\nSELECT * FROM salary_audit ORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>emp_id | old_salary | new_salary | changed_at\n-------+------------+------------+-----------\n8      | 80000      | 85000      | 2024-06-01\n9      | 85000      | 90000      | 2024-06-01</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-x\"></i>Validation with BEFORE + RAISE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TRIGGER trg_no_big_cut\nBEFORE UPDATE OF salary ON employees\nFOR EACH ROW\nWHEN NEW.salary &lt; OLD.salary * 0.8\nBEGIN\n    SELECT RAISE(ABORT, 'salary cut above 20% not allowed');\nEND;\n\nUPDATE employees SET salary = 50000 WHERE emp_id = 2;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Error</figcaption><pre><code>salary cut above 20% not allowed</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calculator\"></i>Maintaining a derived value</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN units_sold INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;\nUPDATE products SET units_sold = (SELECT COALESCE(SUM(quantity), 0) FROM order_items oi\n                                  WHERE oi.product_id = products.product_id);\n\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_units_sold\nAFTER INSERT ON order_items\nFOR EACH ROW\nBEGIN\n    UPDATE products SET units_sold = units_sold + NEW.quantity\n    WHERE product_id = NEW.product_id;\nEND;\n\nINSERT INTO orders VALUES (109, 5, '2024-03-20', 'pending');\nINSERT INTO order_items VALUES (109, 7, 2, 12000), (109, 6, 3, 20);\n\nSELECT name, units_sold FROM products WHERE product_id IN (6, 7) ORDER BY product_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name    | units_sold\n--------+-----------\nPen     | 18\nMonitor | 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-eye-edit\"></i>INSTEAD OF: making a view writable</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE VIEW emp_public AS SELECT emp_id, name, dept_id FROM employees;\n\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_emp_public_insert\nINSTEAD OF INSERT ON emp_public\nBEGIN\n    INSERT INTO employees (emp_id, name, dept_id, salary, hire_date)\n    VALUES (NEW.emp_id, NEW.name, NEW.dept_id, 50000, '2024-06-01');\nEND;\n\nINSERT INTO emp_public VALUES (11, 'Tara', 4);\nSELECT emp_id, name, dept_id, salary FROM employees WHERE emp_id = 11;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>emp_id | name | dept_id | salary\n-------+------+---------+-------\n11     | Tara | 4       | 50000</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>PostgreSQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- PostgreSQL: the trigger body is a function\nCREATE FUNCTION set_updated_at() RETURNS trigger LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$\nBEGIN\n    NEW.updated_at := now();\n    RETURN NEW;\nEND $$;\n\nCREATE TRIGGER trg_touch BEFORE UPDATE ON customers\nFOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION set_updated_at();</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Triggers are invisible side effects: a simple <code>UPDATE</code> can fan out into cascades of hidden writes, slow bulk loads and surprise developers. Keep them small, document them, and prefer constraints for pure integrity rules.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are triggers used for, and what are their drawbacks?</summary><div><p>Audit trails, keeping denormalised/derived columns in sync, enforcing complex rules constraints can't express, and making views writable. Drawbacks: hidden logic, harder debugging, performance cost on every write (especially row-level triggers on bulk operations), and possible trigger chains.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>BEFORE vs AFTER trigger?</summary><div><p><code>BEFORE</code> runs before the row is written — use it to validate or modify <code>NEW</code> values (or reject the change). <code>AFTER</code> runs once the change is applied — use it for side effects like audit rows or updating other tables, when the final values are known.</p></div></details></section>"}